  "query_preview": "..\nLingering moisture this morning will make for a misty start to\nyour Monday. Some areas are experienced patchy dense fog, but \nthis is clearing. A dry northwest wind is forecast by afternoon\nwhich should further open up the sky to sunshine. Temperatures \nare expected to rebound into the 70s this afternoon.\n\nMuch drier air arrives along with the high pressure for tonight\nand Tuesday. With dewpoints dropping into the 30s/40s this \nshould promote a cool night tonight with temperatures most areas\nfalling into the 40s for lows. The mountain valleys across the \nVA Highlands and into the Greenbrier Valley may slip into the \nupper 30s... so it's conceivable to have some patchy frost there\nearly Tuesday morning. Overall however, model output statistical \n(MOS) guidance is a degree or two higher than the cool nights we\nhad a week ago.\n\nTuesday morning should be the coolest, then the numbers trend\nwarmer as the High passes overhead and the airmass modifies.\nHigh temperatures are now advertised in the low-mid 80s for the\npiedmont by Thursday with 70s elsewhere. The week as a whole \nlooks like it is going to be dry, and for the most part, clouds \nshould be at a minimum. Will have to watch to see if any \nmoisture gets trapped underneath the subsidence inversion. \nForecast soundings, especially those near the southern Blue \nRidge suggest a shallow layer of moisture may get trapped \nunderneath for Tuesday and Wednesday. If this occurs, then look\nfor a thin layer of strato-cu to develop. \n\nOur next opportunity for rain may occur next weekend into early\nnext week (i.e. 6-7 days from now). NHC is monitoring a broad \narea of low pressure in the Gulf, and this feature may develop \ninto a cyclone this week. It is possible the system moves ashore\nand eventually brings some moisture to our area. That is a lot \nof ifs, so for now advertising just a chance of rain for the \nsecond half of the weekend and early next week (Oct 11-12).",
